## Summary

Define a trusted, versioned KWin target-input contract so cua-driver can deliver pointer and keyboard events to one verified KDE/KWin target without falling back to global focus-bound input.

Detailed RFC: #3507.

The current KWin helper remains read-only. This issue is the decision record; implementation begins only after a maintainer disposition.

## Core safety contract

The proposed invariant is:

> Before dispatch, failure may be a structured refusal only when zero dispatch is known. Once dispatch may have begun, transport or acknowledgement loss is partial/unknown delivery, preserves any acknowledged progress, and never authorizes replay.

In all cases, selecting the KWin target route must never fall back to global portal/libei input.

The mutation path must also preserve normal Cua Driver per-action permission, manifest/policy, resource, and lifecycle admission. Same-desktop-account transport follows the accepted trusted-local model from #3550/#3551; this proposal does not add a separate KWin-specific permission mode or claim isolation from hostile same-user native code.

## Decisions requested

1. **Exact-target primitive:** which supported Plasma 6/KWin API, if any, can bind mutation to the selected live target at delivery time?
2. **Mutation transport / ownership:** which Driver-owned transport preserves per-action admission, private operation identity, sequence/replay protection, and acknowledgement boundaries without breaking v1 read-only discovery (`GetVersion() == 1`)?
3. **Generation and coordinate binding:** what helper/KWin generation identity and capture/geometry state invalidate stale targets and screenshot-derived positions across restart, PID/token reuse, movement, resize, scale, and transform changes?
4. **Public result mapping:** which shared machine-readable result representation preserves acknowledged progress, possible additional unacknowledged delivery, and the terminal interruption cause without collapsing an in-flight action into a refusal?

If no supported exact-target KWin primitive exists, raw KDE input remains refused.

## Proposed scope

- preserve v1 read-only discovery while negotiating any new target-input capability separately;
- resolve one exact live target using fresh PID/token/generation identity;
- bind screenshot-derived coordinates to the actual capture and target geometry;
- keep hit-testing inside a certified target surface tree; uncertified popup/modal/subsurface roles refuse;
- require normal Driver admission for every mutation, including cached connections;
- use private operation identity plus increasing sequence/replay protection for at-most-once dispatch;
- distinguish definitive zero-dispatch refusal from partial/unknown delivery after dispatch may have begun;
- prohibit automatic replay after timeout, disconnect, helper restart, target re-resolution, or Driver restart;
- bound payloads, queues, action duration, compositor work, concurrency, and cancellation;
- revoke queued/active authority on lifecycle, desktop, keymap, target, owner, helper, or compositor transitions;
- clean operation-owned held state without sending cleanup input to a replacement or unrelated surface;
- route browser existing-profile setup through the same accepted KWin safety boundary;
- report identity-only versus mutation-capable KWin support accurately in doctor/health output.

## Alternatives not accepted without stronger proof

- `activate(target)` followed by global `libei` input;
- sleeps or repeated focus checks around global input;
- title/app-id/geometry-only target lookup;
- `wmctrl` / `xdotool` fallback;
- setting `available() = true` while mutation remains focus-bound;
- treating timeout/disconnect after possible dispatch as a refusal and retrying.

These approaches cannot prove that an irreversible event did not reach the wrong target or was not already delivered once.

## Acceptance evidence

The RFC requires contract, fault-injection, and live Plasma 6 evidence, including:

- stale/duplicate/ambiguous identity, generation/PID reuse, malformed capabilities, and compatibility cases;
- lost-final-reply and duplicate-request tests proving no replay;
- acknowledged-prefix plus unknown-remainder handling for multi-frame operations;
- coordinate crop/scale/transform and stale-geometry cases;
- popup/modal/subsurface target-tree routing;
- focus/user takeover, target loss, owner disconnect, keymap, lock, DPMS, session, helper, and compositor transitions;
- held-key/button cleanup without release to another target;
- queue/resource/concurrency/cancellation bounds and measured public stop latency;
- a live Plasma 6 positive canary proving exact target-addressed pointer and keyboard delivery with a sentinel window proving no leak;
- public result parity across Rust, Python, TypeScript, CLI, MCP, and browser setup paths for every enabled outcome.

Documentation remains experimental until the applicable evidence is recorded.

## Implementation shape after acceptance

Prefer focused implementation PRs beginning with feasibility/transport ownership and capability/identity contracts, followed by routing, browser/health integration, and exact-candidate live evidence.

Related: #3336, #3550, #3551, #2283, #2194.

### Primary area

Cua Driver browser tools

### Summary

Would an explicit **reduced-motion browser policy** be useful for driver-owned isolated Chromium tabs?

The narrow first slice would not inject CSS or change the default browser experience. It would expose the page with:

`prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`

through CDP emulation for an explicitly selected driver-owned tab/session, with a receipt and deterministic cleanup.

### Motivation

For computer-use loops, decorative page motion can create work and screenshot churn between:

`observe -> decide -> act -> verify`

even when the underlying semantic state is unchanged.

I ran into the same general scheduling failure mode in an unrelated gigapixel/deep-zoom experiment: when refinement was allowed to publish during camera motion, interaction tails were much worse; moving that non-critical work outside the motion window materially improved worst frames.

That is **not CUA evidence**, so I think this should start as a benchmarkable opt-in capability rather than a default optimization.

### Proposed first behavior

Only for driver-owned isolated Chromium in v0:

- caller explicitly requests `prefer_reduced_motion`;
- apply the standard reduced-motion media preference to the bound tab;
- bind the policy to current tab/document identity;
- preserve it across navigation only while the explicit session policy remains active;
- session/tab teardown restores default behavior;
- receipt reports requested/effective policy and document generation;
- no CSS injection;
- no timer/video/canvas/WebGL freezing;
- no existing-profile mutation in the first slice.

### Fixture / acceptance

Use a deterministic page containing:

- CSS transitions/animations;
- smooth scrolling;
- an action followed by a DOM update;
- one decorative-animation case;
- one animation-dependent semantic case.

Compare baseline vs reduced-motion:

- semantic result/action parity;
- screenshot hash churn while semantic state is unchanged;
- `semantic_observe_ms`;
- screenshot/capture latency;
- verification/settle latency;
- `total_step_ms`;
- stale ref/snapshot outcomes.

A speedup does not count if semantics change.

### Why this fits the current browser architecture

The semantic snapshot work from @f-trycua in #2299 already distinguishes current visible/actionable state from irrelevant retained state; this would apply the same "only spend work on what matters to the current decision" principle to cooperative page motion.

@antoniel's #3971 also gives useful evidence that post-action settle time can dominate otherwise-fast state changes. This proposal is orthogonal: rather than globally shortening waits, test whether the page can cooperatively produce a more stable observable state sooner.

The broader downstream experiment, including a stronger CSS-suppression arm, is tracked here:

https://github.com/kvnloo/cua/issues/109

If this direction is useful, I'd keep the first implementation to the reduced-motion media emulation only.

### Primary area

Cua Driver

### Summary

`get_browser_state` (`semantic_v2`) can loop forever inside `SemanticDocument::page` when the page has an out-of-process iframe. Each stuck call keeps one daemon worker thread at about 100% CPU indefinitely. The client times out, but the work never stops, and `cua-driver stop` cannot stop the daemon.

In normal use we found four such calls that had been running for about 32 hours. Together they had used about 110 CPU-hours, and `cua-driver serve` sat at 300–330% CPU.

### Reproduction

It reproduces deterministically with two local pages and a short script. The script uses DevTools to line up DOM node ids the way they lined up by chance on the real page. All the files are below.

1. Serve the two pages with `python3 -m http.server 8765 --bind 127.0.0.1`. The main page is loaded from `http://localhost:8765` and the iframe from `http://127.0.0.1:8765`, so the iframe is cross-site and runs out of process.
2. Run `cua-driver call browser_prepare '{"session":"r","allow_launch":true,"profile":{"mode":"isolated_new"}}'`.
3. Bind the new window with `list_windows` and `get_browser_state {"pid":…,"window_id":…,"session":"r"}`.
4. Call `browser_navigate` to `http://localhost:8765/main.html`.
5. Run `node arrange.mjs <port>`, where `<port>` is the first line of the isolated profile's `DevToolsActivePort`. It prints `ARRANGED`.
6. Run `cua-driver call get_browser_state '{"target_id":"…","tab_id":"…","session":"r","snapshot_format":"semantic_v2"}'`.

The call in step 6 never returns. With a 60-second client timeout, one thread of `cua-driver serve` stayed at 80–90% CPU throughout and kept running after the client gave up. The only way to stop it is to kill the daemon.

On real pages it is intermittent, because it needs ids to collide in this pattern across a cross-site iframe. We hit it once in normal use, on a public sign-up page with two cross-site iframes (a tracking sync frame and a chat widget), after an agent had been working on the page for about half an hour. One call hung and three retries hung the same way.

<details><summary><code>main.html</code> and <code>frame.html</code></summary>

```html
<!-- main.html -->
<!doctype html><html><body><h1>cua-driver loop repro</h1><button aria-label="main">main</button><iframe src="http://127.0.0.1:8765/frame.html" style="width:600px;height:300px"></iframe></body></html>
```

`frame.html` holds 400 buttons, which moves the iframe process's id counter well above the main page's:

```sh
python3 -c 'print("<!doctype html><html><body>" + "".join(f"<div role=\"none\"><button aria-label=\"f{i}\"></button></div>" for i in range(400)) + "</body></html>")' > frame.html
```

</details>

<details><summary><code>arrange.mjs</code> (Node 22+, built-in WebSocket)</summary>

```js
// Usage: node arrange.mjs <devtools-port>
// Iframe: its <body> D (AX: ignored "uninteresting", kept in the tree) holds a
// kept <button> B. Main frame: a kept <button> C takes D's id inside a wrapper
// W that takes B's id. cua-driver drops ignored D, so after the merge
// C -> W's id resolves to the iframe's B -> B's parent id resolves to main C.
// DOM node ids are per renderer process and handed out on first request, so
// the script spends ids in the main process until its counter lines up.
const port = process.argv[2];

function session(wsUrl) {
  const ws = new WebSocket(wsUrl);
  let id = 0;
  const pending = new Map();
  ws.onmessage = (event) => {
    const msg = JSON.parse(event.data);
    if (msg.id && pending.has(msg.id)) {
      const { resolve, reject } = pending.get(msg.id);
      pending.delete(msg.id);
      msg.error ? reject(new Error(JSON.stringify(msg.error))) : resolve(msg.result);
    }
  };
  const ready = new Promise((resolve, reject) => ((ws.onopen = resolve), (ws.onerror = reject)));
  const call = (method, params = {}) =>
    new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const n = ++id;
      pending.set(n, { resolve, reject });
      ws.send(JSON.stringify({ id: n, method, params }));
    });
  return { ready, call, close: () => ws.close() };
}

const targets = await (await fetch(`http://127.0.0.1:${port}/json/list`)).json();
const main = session(targets.find((t) => t.type === "page" && t.url.includes("localhost:8765")).webSocketDebuggerUrl);
const frame = session(targets.find((t) => t.type === "iframe" && t.url.includes("127.0.0.1:8765")).webSocketDebuggerUrl);
await Promise.all([main.ready, frame.ready]);

async function idOf(s, expression, depth = 0) {
  const { result } = await s.call("Runtime.evaluate", { expression });
  const { node } = await s.call("DOM.describeNode", { objectId: result.objectId, depth });
  return Number(node.backendNodeId);
}
const tick = () => idOf(main, `document.body.appendChild(document.createElement('i'))`);
const spend = (n) =>
  idOf(main, `(() => { const c = document.createElement('section'); c.hidden = true; for (let i = 0; i < ${n}; i++) c.append(document.createElement('i')); document.body.append(c); return c; })()`, -1);

const d = await idOf(frame, `document.body`);
const b = await idOf(frame, `(() => { const b = document.createElement('button'); b.setAttribute('aria-label', 'frame-B'); document.body.prepend(b); return b; })()`);
await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 300));
const fax = (await frame.call("Accessibility.getFullAXTree")).nodes;
const axD = fax.find((n) => n.nodeId === String(d));
const axB = fax.find((n) => n.nodeId === String(b));
if (!axD?.ignored || axB?.parentId !== String(d)) throw new Error("iframe shape not as needed");

let next = await tick();
if (next >= d) throw new Error(`main counter ${next} already past ${d}; use a fresh browser`);
if (d - next - 12 > 0) await spend(d - next - 12);
next = await tick();
while (next < d - 1) next = await tick();
const c = await idOf(main, `(() => { const c = document.createElement('button'); c.id = 'C'; c.setAttribute('aria-label', 'main-C'); document.body.prepend(c); return c; })()`);
next = c;
while (next < b - 1) next = await tick();
const w = await idOf(main, `(() => { const w = document.createElement('div'); w.id = 'W'; const c = document.getElementById('C'); c.replaceWith(w); w.append(c); return w; })()`);
await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 300));
const axC = (await main.call("Accessibility.getFullAXTree")).nodes.find((n) => n.nodeId === String(c));
const arranged = c === d && w === b && axC?.parentId === String(b);
console.log(`iframe D=${d} B=${b}; main C=${c} W=${w} -> ${arranged ? "ARRANGED" : "NOT ARRANGED"}`);
main.close();
frame.close();
process.exit(arranged ? 0 : 1);
```

