### Summary
On Linux X11, when a window has no AT-SPI tree, `get_window_state` falls back to X11 properties and returns one `window` row with `element_index: 0`, `actions: ["activate"]` and an `element_token`. Passing that token straight to `press_key` or `click` in the same session is refused with `invalid_element_token`, because the snapshot that issued it has no rows.

### Reproduction
1. On an X11 desktop, build and start a plain Xlib window with no accessibility bridge (`fallback.c` below logs each KeyPress to a file):
   `cc fallback.c -o fallback-input-probe -lX11 && ./fallback-input-probe /tmp/keys.txt &`
2. `cua-driver serve --socket /tmp/driver.sock`
3. `cua-driver call --socket /tmp/driver.sock list_windows '{"pid": PID}'` (PID of the fixture) and take its `window_id`.
4. `python3 reproduce.py "$(command -v cua-driver)" /tmp/driver.sock PID WINDOW_ID /tmp/keys.txt /tmp/out`

`reproduce.py` makes one `get_window_state` (`tree_format: "elements"`) in session `fallback-repro`. It then immediately sends the returned row's `element_token` to `press_key` (`key: "a"`, `delivery_mode: "foreground"`) with the same pid and window_id, then sends `b` without a token as a control. It fails on every attempt.

